## Yes – you can draw contour lines **directly from a `raster-dem` source at runtime**  
MapLibre GL JS does not have a built-in “contour” layer type, but the **`maplibre-contour`** library does the heavy lifting for you:

* It reads the same DEM tiles you already use for hillshade / 3-D terrain.  
* In a Web-Worker it extracts elevation values, runs a marching-squares algorithm and emits **vector tiles** that contain the contour geometry.  
* Those vector tiles are then added to the map like any other `vector` source and styled with a normal `line` (or `symbol`) layer.

Below is a step-by-step guide, a complete working example, and a few gotchas to keep in mind.

---

## 1️⃣ Install the library

```bash
# npm / yarn / pnpm
npm install maplibre-contour   # or: yarn add maplibre-contour
```

> **Why a separate library?**  
> The contour extraction is CPU-intensive. `maplibre-contour` runs it in a Web-Worker so the UI stays smooth, and it follows the same style-spec conventions as native MapLibre layers.

---

## 2️⃣ Prepare your DEM source

Your DEM must already be usable by MapLibre (i.e. a `raster-dem` source).  
Make sure you know the **encoding** that the tiles use – the two most common are:

| Encoding | What the pixel values mean |
|----------|----------------------------|
| `terrarium` | `R*256 + G + B/256 – 32768` (meters) |
| `mapbox`    | `((R*256 + G)*256 + B) / 10 – 10000` (meters) |

If you are not sure, check the provider’s documentation or the TileJSON you are loading.

```js
// Example DEM source (same one you’ll use for hillshade / terrain)
map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  url: 'https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/{z}/{x}/{y}.webp', // any raster-dem endpoint
  tileSize: 256,
  // optional: maxzoom, attribution, etc.
});
```

---

## 3️⃣ Create a `DemSource` from `maplibre-contour`

```js
import mlcontour from 'maplibre-contour';

// 1️⃣  Build the DEM wrapper – it knows how to fetch tiles and decode them.
const demSource = new mlcontour.DemSource({
  // URL pattern must match the raster-dem source above
  url: 'https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/{z}/{x}/{y}.webp',
  // Must match the DEM encoding you are using
  encoding: 'terrarium',   // ← change if your DEM uses Mapbox encoding
  maxzoom: 12,             // highest zoom you want contours for
  // Set to true to run the worker in a separate thread (recommended)
  worker: true
});

// 2️⃣  Register the wrapper with the MapLibre instance.
//     This adds a custom protocol (`mlcontour://`) that the map can request.
demSource.setupMaplibre(maplibregl);
```

> **What `setupMaplibre` does**  
> It registers a `mlcontour://` URL scheme that resolves to on-the-fly generated vector tiles. You never have to host a separate vector tileset.

---

## 4️⃣ Add a *virtual* vector source that points to the contour protocol

```js
// The protocol URL is built for each zoom level you care about.
// `thresholds` tells the worker how often to emit lines (in metres).
// `elevationKey` is the property name that will hold the raw elevation value.
// `levelKey` is the property that tells you whether a line is a “major” contour.
map.addSource('contours', {
  type: 'vector',
  // The protocol URL is a *template* – the worker will replace {z}/{x}/{y}
  // with the tile coordinates it is asked for.
  tiles: [
    demSource.contourProtocolUrl({
      thresholds: {
        // zoom : [minor interval, major interval]
        11: [200, 1000],
        12: [100, 500],
        14: [50, 200]
      },
      elevationKey: 'ele',   // name of the property that holds the height (m)
      levelKey: 'level',     // 1 = major contour, 0 = minor
      contourLayer: 'contours' // name of the internal vector layer
    })
  ],
  // The worker can generate tiles up to this zoom; beyond that MapLibre will
  // fall back to the highest-resolution tiles it has.
  maxzoom: 15
});
```

### How the `thresholds` work

* At a given zoom the worker will generate **two** sets of lines:
  * **Minor** (e.g. every 100 m) – usually drawn thin and faint.
  * **Major** (e.g. every 500 m) – drawn thicker or labelled.
* You can change the intervals per zoom to keep the map readable at different scales.

---

## 5️⃣ Style the contour lines

Now treat the source like any other vector source:

```js
// Minor contours – thin, semi-transparent
map.addLayer({
  id: 'contour-minor',
  type: 'line',
  source: 'contours',
  'source-layer': 'contours', // matches the `contourLayer` name above
  filter: ['==', ['get', 'level'], 0], // only minor lines
  paint: {
    'line-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.4)',
    'line-width': 0.8,
    'line-dasharray': [2, 2] // optional dash for minor lines
  }
});

// Major contours – thicker, solid
map.addLayer({
  id: 'contour-major',
  type: 'line',
  source: 'contours',
  'source-layer': 'contours',
  filter: ['==', ['get', 'level'], 1],
  paint: {
    'line-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.7)',
    'line-width': 1.5
  }
});
```

### Optional: label the major contours

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'contour-labels',
  type: 'symbol',
  source: 'contours',
  'source-layer': 'contours',
  filter: ['==', ['get', 'level'], 1],
  layout: {
    'text-field': ['format',
      ['get', 'ele'], { 'font-scale': 0.9 }, // elevation value
      '\n', {},                               // line break
      ['get', 'level'], { 'font-scale': 0.6 } // optional “major” flag
    ],
    'text-font': ['Open Sans Regular', 'Arial Unicode MS Regular'],
    'text-size': 12,
    'text-offset': [0, 0.5],
    'text-anchor': 'top'
  },
  paint: {
    'text-color': '#222',
    'text-halo-color': '#fff',
    'text-halo-width': 1
  }
});
```

---

## 6️⃣ Full Minimal Example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre runtime contours</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <script type="module">
    import mlcontour from 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/maplibre-contour@2.0.0/+esm';

    const map = new maplibregl.Map({
      container: 'map',
      style: {
        version: 8,
        sources: {},
        layers: [{ id: 'background', type: 'background', paint: { 'background-color': '#e0e0e0' } }]
      },
      center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
      zoom: 12,
      pitch: 45,
      bearing: -17
    });

    map.on('load', () => {
      // 1️⃣ DEM source (used for terrain & hillshade if you want)
      map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
        type: 'raster-dem',
        url: 'https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/{z}/{x}/{y}.webp',
        tileSize: 256
      });

      // 2️⃣ Optional: 3-D terrain so you can see the relief underneath
      map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1.5 });
      map.setSky({ 'sky-color': '#88c0d0', 'horizon-color': '#fff', 'fog-color': '#c0d0ff' });

      // 3️⃣ Build the contour generator
      const demSource = new mlcontour.DemSource({
        url: 'https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/{z}/{x}/{y}.webp',
        encoding: 'terrarium',
        maxzoom: 12,
        worker: true
      });
      demSource.setupMaplibre(maplibregl);

      // 4️⃣ Add the virtual vector source
      map.addSource('contours', {
        type: 'vector',
        tiles: [
          demSource.contourProtocolUrl({
            thresholds: { 11: [200, 1000], 12: [100, 500], 14: [50, 200] },
            elevationKey: 'ele',
            levelKey: 'level',
            contourLayer: 'contours'
          })
        ],
        maxzoom: 15
      });

      // 5️⃣ Style the lines
      map.addLayer({
        id: 'contour-minor',
        type: 'line',
        source: 'contours',
        'source-layer': 'contours',
        filter: ['==', ['get', 'level'], 0],
        paint: {
          'line-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.4)',
          'line-width': 0.8,
          'line-dasharray': [2, 2]
        }
      });

      map.addLayer({
        id: 'contour-major',
        type: 'line',
        source: 'contours',
        'source-layer': 'contours',
        filter: ['==', ['get', 'level'], 1],
        paint: {
          'line-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.7)',
          'line-width': 1.5
        }
      });

      // 6️⃣ Optional labels
      map.addLayer({
        id: 'contour-labels',
        type: 'symbol',
        source: 'contours',
        'source-layer': 'contours',
        filter: ['==', ['get', 'level'], 1],
        layout: {
          'text-field': ['format', ['get', 'ele'], { 'font-scale': 0.9 }],
          'text-size': 12,
          'text-offset': [0, 0.5],
          'text-anchor': 'top'
        },
        paint: {
          'text-color': '#222',
          'text-halo-color': '#fff',
          'text-halo-width': 1
        }
      });
    });
  </script>
  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the file in a browser (or serve it via a local web server) and you’ll see:

* 3-D terrain (optional) with a sky.
* Contour lines generated **on-the-fly** from the same DEM tiles.
* Minor/major styling that adapts as you zoom.

---

## 7️⃣ Things to Keep in Mind

| Issue | Why it matters | How to address |
|-------|----------------|----------------|
| **Performance** | Contour extraction is CPU-heavy, especially at high zooms. | • Keep `maxzoom` modest (e.g. 12-13). <br>• Use the Web-Worker (`worker: true`). <br>• Limit the number of zoom levels in `thresholds`. |
| **Encoding mismatch** | If you tell the library the wrong encoding, elevations will be garbage → wrong contour spacing. | Verify the DEM provider’s docs. Test a single tile with `map.queryTerrainElevation([lng,lat])` and compare to known values. |
| **Tile-size / format** | `raster-dem` can be `png`, `webp`, `jpeg`, etc. The library can decode all common formats, but some browsers may not support WebP in workers. | Use PNG/WEBP that the browser can decode; fallback to PNG if you see “no contours”. |
| **CORS** | The DEM tiles must be CORS-enabled (or served from the same origin) because the worker fetches them directly. | Ensure the tile server sends `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your domain). |
|

## Yes – you can draw contour lines **directly from a `raster-dem` source at runtime**  
MapLibre GL JS does not have a built-in “contour” layer type, but the **`maplibre-contour`** library does the heavy lifting for you:

* It reads the same DEM tiles you already use for hillshade / 3-D terrain.  
* In a Web-Worker it extracts elevation values, runs a marching-squares algorithm and emits **vector tiles** that contain the contour geometry.  
* Those vector tiles are then added to the map like any other `vector` source and styled with a normal `line` (or `symbol`) layer.

Below is a step-by-step guide, a complete working example, and a few gotchas to keep in mind.

---

## 1️⃣ Install the library

```bash
# npm / yarn / pnpm
npm install maplibre-contour   # or: yarn add maplibre-contour
```

> **Why a separate library?**  
> The contour extraction is CPU-intensive. `maplibre-contour` runs it in a Web-Worker so the UI stays smooth, and it follows the same style-spec conventions as native MapLibre layers.

---

## 2️⃣ Prepare your DEM source

Your DEM must already be usable by MapLibre (i.e. a `raster-dem` source).  
Make sure you know the **encoding** that the tiles use – the two most common are:

| Encoding | How the elevation is packed | Typical source |
|----------|----------------------------|----------------|
| `terrarium` |  (R × 256 + G + B/256) − 32768  | MapTiler, Terrarium DEMs |
| `mapbox`    |  (R × 256 + G + B) × 0.1 − 10000 | Mapbox Terrain-RGB |

You’ll need to pass the same string to `DemSource` (see step 3).

```js
// Example DEM source – you probably already have this in your style
map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  url: 'https://tiles.example.com/terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
  // optional: tileSize, maxzoom, etc.
});
```

---

## 3️⃣ Create a `DemSource` (the contour generator)

```js
import mlcontour from 'maplibre-contour';

// 1️⃣  Create the DEM-to-contour helper
const demSource = new mlcontour.DemSource({
  // URL pattern must match the raster-dem tiles you use above
  url: 'https://tiles.example.com/terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
  // Must match the encoding of those tiles
  encoding: 'terrarium',          // or 'mapbox'
  maxzoom: 12,                    // highest zoom you want contours for
  // Set to true to run the worker in a separate thread (recommended)
  worker: true
});

// 2️⃣  Register the helper with the MapLibre instance
demSource.setupMaplibre(maplibregl);
```

### What `DemSource` does

| Step | What happens |
|------|--------------|
| **Tile fetch** | The same DEM tiles are requested (cached by the browser). |
| **Decode** | The worker decodes the RGB values into meters using the chosen `encoding`. |
| **Contour extraction** | For each zoom you request, the worker runs marching-squares and creates a **vector tile** (MVT) with two properties: <br>• `ele` – the elevation of the line (in meters) <br>• `level` – `1` for major (e.g. 100 m) lines, `0` for minor (e.g. 20 m) lines. |
| **Protocol URL** | The generated tiles are served through a custom protocol (`mlcontour://…`) that MapLibre can read as a normal vector source. |

---

## 4️⃣ Add a *virtual* vector source that points at the contour protocol

```js
// The protocol URL is built for each zoom level you care about.
// You can change the thresholds (spacing) per zoom – see the docs.
map.addSource('contours', {
  type: 'vector',
  // The protocol URL is a *template*; the library expands {z}/{x}/{y}
  // when the map asks for a tile.
  tiles: [
    demSource.contourProtocolUrl({
      // Zoom → [minorSpacing, majorSpacing] (both in meters)
      thresholds: {
        11: [200, 1000],
        12: [100, 500],
        13: [50, 200],
        14: [20, 100],
        15: [10, 50]
      },
      // Property names that will appear on each feature
      elevationKey: 'ele',   // elevation value (meters)
      levelKey: 'level',     // 0 = minor, 1 = major
      // Optional: give the layer a name (used in source-layer)
      contourLayer: 'contours'
    })
  ],
  // The vector source must know its maxzoom (same as the DEM)
  maxzoom: 15
});
```

> **Why a custom protocol?**  
> The library creates the vector tiles on-the-fly, so there is no real URL to fetch. The protocol (`mlcontour://…`) tells MapLibre “hand this request to the worker and give me back a tile”.

---

## 5️⃣ Style the contour lines

Now you can add a normal `line` layer (or a `symbol` layer for labels) that reads from the virtual source.

```js
// 1️⃣  Simple line styling
map.addLayer({
  id: 'contour-lines',
  type: 'line',
  source: 'contours',
  'source-layer': 'contours',   // matches the `contourLayer` name above
  paint: {
    // Dark thin lines for minor contours, thicker/brighter for majors
    'line-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.6)',
    'line-width': [
      'match',
      ['get', 'level'],   // 1 = major, 0 = minor
      1, 1.5,              // major → 1.5px
      0, 0.8,              // minor → 0.8px
      0.5                  // fallback
    ],
    'line-dasharray': [
      'match',
      ['get', 'level'],
      1, [2, 2],   // major = solid
      0, [1, 2],   // minor = dashed
      [1, 2]       // fallback
    ]
  }
});
```

### Optional: label the major contours

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'contour-labels',
  type: 'symbol',
  source: 'contours',
  'source-layer': 'contours',
  filter: ['==', ['get', 'level'], 1],   // only major lines
  layout: {
    'symbol-placement': 'line',
    'text-field': ['to-string', ['get', 'ele']],
    'text-font': ['Open Sans Bold', 'Arial Unicode MS Bold'],
    'text-size': 12,
    'text-allow-overlap': false,
    'text-ignore-placement': false
  },
  paint: {
    'text-color': '#222',
    'text-halo-color': '#fff',
    'text-halo-width': 1
  }
});
```

---

