## 7️⃣ Things to Keep in Mind

| Issue | Why it matters | How to address |
|-------|----------------|----------------|
| **Performance** | Contour extraction is CPU-heavy, especially at high zooms. | • Use `worker: true` (default). <br>• Limit `maxzoom` to the highest zoom you actually need contours for (e.g. 14-15). <br>• Choose coarser `thresholds` at low zooms (larger spacing). |
| **Encoding mismatch** | If you tell the library the wrong encoding, elevations will be garbage → wrong contour spacing. | Verify the DEM source’s documentation (Terrarium vs Mapbox). Test a single tile with `console.log(demSource.decodeTile(...))` if you’re unsure. |
| **Cache** | The same DEM tile may be decoded many times (once per zoom level). | The library internally caches decoded DEM tiles; you can also enable HTTP caching on the tile server. |
| **Styling limits** | Only the two properties (`elevationKey`, `levelKey`) are guaranteed. | If you need more (e.g. slope, aspect) you’d have to fork the library and add extra attributes in the worker. |
| **Browser support** | Web-Workers + `fetch` + `ArrayBuffer

## Yes – you can draw contour lines **directly from a `raster-dem` source at runtime**  
MapLibre GL JS does not have a built-in “contour” layer type, but the **`maplibre-contour`** library does the heavy lifting for you:

* It reads the same DEM tiles you already use for hillshade / 3-D terrain.  
* In a Web-Worker it extracts elevation values, runs a marching-squares algorithm and emits **vector tiles** that contain the contour geometry.  
* Those vector tiles are then added to the map like any other `vector` source and styled with a normal `line` (or `symbol`) layer.

Below is a step-by-step guide, a complete working example, and a few gotchas to keep in mind.

---

## 1️⃣ Install the library

```bash
# npm / yarn / pnpm
npm install maplibre-contour   # or: yarn add maplibre-contour
```

> **Why a separate library?**  
> The contour extraction is CPU-intensive. `maplibre-contour` runs it in a Web-Worker so the UI stays smooth, and it follows the same style-spec conventions as native MapLibre layers.

---

## 2️⃣ Prepare your DEM source

Your DEM must already be usable by MapLibre (i.e. a `raster-dem` source).  
Make sure you know the **encoding** that the tiles use – the two most common are:

| Encoding | How the elevation is packed | Typical source |
|----------|----------------------------|----------------|
| `terrarium` |  (R × 256 + G + B/256) − 32768  | Mapbox-style DEMs |
| `mapbox`    |  (R × 256 + G + B) / 10          | Mapbox DEM tiles |

You will pass this same string to `maplibre-contour` so it can decode the values correctly.

```js
const demSource = new maplibreContour.DemSource({
  // URL pattern for the DEM tiles (same as you use for hillshade/terrain)
  url: 'https://tiles.example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.webp',
  // Must match the source’s encoding
  encoding: 'terrarium',   // or 'mapbox'
  maxzoom: 12,             // highest zoom you have DEM tiles for
  // Optional: run the worker in a separate thread (highly recommended)
  worker: true
});
```

> **Tip:** If you already added the DEM as a `raster-dem` source in the style, you can reuse the same URL pattern – you don’t need a second source in the style.

---

## 3️⃣ Hook the library into MapLibre

```js
// `map` is your MapLibre GL JS Map instance
demSource.setupMaplibre(maplibregl);
```

`setupMaplibre` registers a custom protocol (`mlcontour://`) that the library uses to serve the generated contour tiles.

---

## 4️⃣ Add a *virtual* vector source that points to the contour protocol

```js
map.addSource('contours', {
  type: 'vector',
  // The protocol URL is built by the library; you can pass options here.
  tiles: [
    demSource.contourProtocolUrl({
      // ── Contour generation options ────────────────────────
      // Elevation value that will be stored on each feature (default: 'ele')
      elevationKey: 'ele',
      // Property name for the contour level (major/minor) – used for styling
      levelKey: 'level',
      // The name of the source-layer that will appear in the vector tiles
      contourLayer: 'contours',
      // ── Zoom-dependent contour spacing (in metres) ────────
      //   { zoom: [minorInterval, majorInterval] }
      thresholds: {
        11: [200, 1000],
        12: [100, 500],
        14: [50, 200],
        15: [20, 100]
      }
    })
  ],
  // The highest zoom you want to request contour tiles for.
  // (Usually a few levels higher than the DEM maxzoom so the lines stay crisp.)
  maxzoom: 15
});
```

**What the options mean**

| Option | Description |
|--------|-------------|
| `elevationKey` | Property that holds the raw elevation (in metres) for each contour feature. |
| `levelKey` | Property that tells whether the line is a *major* contour (usually `1`) or a *minor* one (`0`). Useful for styling different widths or labels. |
| `contourLayer` | The name of the source-layer inside the generated vector tiles. You’ll reference this in `source-layer` when you add a layer. |
| `thresholds` | Controls how dense the contours are at each zoom. The first number is the **minor** interval, the second the **major** interval. Adjust to your data’s vertical resolution and visual preference. |
| `maxzoom` (source) | The highest zoom you’ll request from the protocol. Keep it modest (≤ 15) – higher values generate a lot of work for the worker. |

---

## 5️⃣ Style the contour lines

Now add a normal `line` layer (or a `symbol` layer for labels) that reads from the virtual source:

```js
// Simple line styling – thin minor lines, thicker major lines
map.addLayer({
  id: 'contour-lines',
  type: 'line',
  source: 'contours',
  'source-layer': 'contours',   // matches the `contourLayer` name above
  paint: {
    // Dark semi-transparent lines
    'line-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.6)',
    // Vary width based on whether it’s a major contour (level === 1)
    'line-width': [
      'match',
      ['get', 'level'],
      1, 2.0,   // major contour → 2 px
      0, 0.8,   // minor contour → 0.8 px
      0.5       // fallback
    ]
  }
});
```

### Optional: label the major contours

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'contour-labels',
  type: 'symbol',
  source: 'contours',
  'source-layer': 'contours',
  filter: ['==', ['get', 'level'], 1],   // only major contours
  layout: {
    'symbol-placement': 'line',
    'text-field': ['format',
      ['get', 'ele'], { 'text-font': ['Open Sans Bold'] },
      '\n', {} ,
      ['concat', ['to-string', ['/', ['get', 'ele'], 1000]], ' km'],
      { 'text-font': ['Open Sans Regular'], 'text-size': 10 }
    ],
    'text-size': 12,
    'text-allow-overlap': false,
    'text-ignore-placement': false
  },
  paint: {
    'text-color': '#222',
    'text-halo-color': '#fff',
    'text-halo-width': 1
  }
});
```

> **Why use `filter`?**  
> The label layer only needs the *major* contours (the ones you want to read). Filtering reduces the number of label candidates and improves performance.

---

## 6️⃣ Full minimal example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre runtime contours</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <script type="module">
    import maplibreContour from 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/maplibre-contour@2.0.0/+esm';

    const map = new maplibregl.Map({
      container: 'map',
      style: {
        version: 8,
        sources: {
          // The DEM source that will also feed the contour generator
          'terrain-dem': {
            type: 'raster-dem',
            url: 'https://tiles.example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.webp',
            tileSize: 256,
            maxzoom: 12
          },
          // A simple raster imagery source (optional)
          'sat': {
            type: 'raster',
            url: 'https://tiles.example.com/sat/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
            tileSize: 256
          }
        },
        layers: [
          { id: 'sat', type: 'raster', source: 'sat' }
        ]
      },
      center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
      zoom: 12,
      pitch: 45,
      bearing: -17,
      antialias: true
    });

    map.on('load', async () => {
      // 1️⃣ Create the DEM source for the contour worker
      const demSource = new maplibreContour.DemSource({
        url: 'https://tiles.example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.webp',
        encoding: 'terrarium',
        maxzoom: 12,
        worker: true
      });
      demSource.setupMaplibre(maplibregl);

      // 2️⃣ Add the virtual vector source that serves contour tiles
      map.addSource('contours', {
        type: 'vector',
        tiles: [
          demSource.contourProtocolUrl({
            elevationKey: 'ele',
            levelKey: 'level',
            contourLayer: 'contours',
            thresholds: {
              11: [200, 1000],
              12: [100, 500],
              14: [50, 200],
              15: [20, 100]
            }
          })
        ],
        maxzoom: 15
      });

      // 3️⃣ Draw the lines
      map.addLayer({
        id: 'contour-lines',
        type: 'line',
        source: 'contours',
        'source-layer': 'contours',
        paint: {
          'line-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.6)',
          'line-width': [
            'match',
            ['get', 'level'],
            1, 2,
            0, 0.8,
            0.5
          ]
        }
      });

      // 4️⃣ (Optional) Labels for major contours
      map.addLayer({
        id: 'contour-labels',
        type: 'symbol',
        source: 'contours',
        'source-layer': 'contours',
        filter: ['==', ['get', 'level'], 1],
        layout: {
          'symbol-placement': 'line',
          'text-field': ['format',
            ['to-string', ['get', 'ele']],
            { 'text-font': ['Open Sans Bold'] },
            '\n', {},
            ['concat', ['to-string', ['/', ['get', 'ele'], 1000]], ' km'],
            { 'text-font': ['Open Sans Regular'], 'text-size': 10 }
          ],
          'text-size': 12,
          'text-allow-overlap': false
        },
        paint: {
          'text-color': '#222',
          'text-halo-color': '#fff',
          'text-halo-width': 1
        }
      });
    });
  </script>
  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
</body>
</html>
```

*Open the page in a modern browser (Chrome, Edge, Firefox, Safari) and you’ll see the raster imagery draped over the DEM, with contour lines generated on-the-fly.*  

---

## 7️⃣ Things to watch out for

| Issue | Why it happens | Fix / Mitigation |
|-------|----------------|------------------|
| **No contours appear** | The DEM encoding (`terrarium` vs `mapbox`) is wrong, or the DEM tiles are missing at the requested zoom. | Verify the DEM source works for hillshade/terrain first. Use the same `encoding` string in `DemSource`. |
| **Very slow initial load** | The worker must decode many DEM tiles and run marching-squares for each zoom level. | • Keep `maxzoom` for the DEM modest (≤ 12). <br>• Use `worker: true` (default) to off-load to a background thread. <br>• Cache the generated vector tiles by setting `map.setMaxTileCacheSize()` if you need repeated pans/zooms. |
| **Contours look “jagged” at high zoom** | The algorithm works on the DEM resolution; if the DEM is coarse, the contour geometry inherits that coarseness. | Use a higher-resolution DEM (or a higher `maxzoom`) or accept a smoother look by increasing the `exaggeration` of the terrain (does not affect contour geometry, only visual perception). |
| **Too many lines clutter the map** | `thresholds` are too fine (e.g., 10 m intervals
